; Provider-route executable family, 5 of 7 — `:scope` composes.
;
; Each `(pass …)` opens its own registry, so the two shaders' extracted
; names do not see each other even though both went through the same
; provider. Three things are pinned at once:
;
;   * within a pass, a reference to that pass's extracted name resolves;
;   * across passes it does not — `u_b` is a real name in the *other*
;     pass, which is precisely the mistake scoping exists to catch, and
;     it reads as an ordinary `not_cross_ref`;
;   * outside every pass there is no registry to consult at all, which is
;     `cross_ref_outside_scope` rather than a miss.
;
; The extraction table itself is scope-blind — it is content-addressed by
; `(provider, source)` — so the scoping happens entirely at registration.
; Two passes carrying the *same* source would share one extraction and
; still land in two registries.
(use-plugin "lines")

(pass
  (shader :name a :src "u_a")
  (bind :uniform u_a)
  (bind :uniform u_b))

(pass
  (shader :name b :src "u_b")
  (bind :uniform u_b))

(bind :uniform u_a)
